WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:11.940
36. Asperger's Syndrome. Adam is very, very smart. He has an excellent memory and

2
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:17.700
astonishes people with the information he can recall. But he didn't have many

3
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:23.460
friends at school. He couldn't figure out how to have a conversation. When was it

4
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.700
his turn to talk? When should he stop? What did it mean when people's faces

5
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:34.580
changed expression? He couldn't understand these things. Wendy has always hated

6
00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:40.300
loud sounds. Light also bothers her, and she only likes to wear very soft clothes.

7
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.140
She loved learning about insects, animals, and medicine, but hated school, where she

8
00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:52.420
was bullied. Wendy and Adam have Asperger's Syndrome. It's a type of autism, a

9
00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.780
disorder that still hasn't been well understood. We don't know why some people

10
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:02.940
develop it. We're only now learning how to help people with autism and Asperger's

11
00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:08.160
interact with the world. Preventing the disorder, let alone curing it, which is

12
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:14.300
controversial, is still far in the future. People with Asperger's generally seem

13
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:19.580
more normal than other autistic people. They learn well, can speak normally, and

14
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:25.900
are usually very smart. Social interaction is their problem. It is hard

15
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:31.340
for them to read body language and understand social rules like when to talk.

16
00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:36.700
People with Asperger's may not understand small talk, but may ramble on about a

17
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.980
particular interest, whether or not their listeners care. How to move can also be a

18
00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:46.980
problem. They may not use normal eye contact, or they might require more space

19
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:52.960
than other people. They may not seem emotional or affectionate. People with

20
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.480
Asperger's do feel, of course. They just show it differently. They think differently

21
00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.680
than most people, but they think deeply and carefully. Our challenge for the

22
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.720
future will be to learn to understand each other so we can use all of the

23
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:13.040
different gifts different people are given.
